<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="SecondDegreeFactor">
  <title>Factoring: Second Degree Expression</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Recall the distribution rules from <xref ref="Distribution">Section</xref>
      <xref ref="DistributionExample5">Example</xref>:
      <md>
        <mrow>(x+2)(x+3) \amp  = x(x+3)+2(x+3)</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp  = x^2+3x+2x+6</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp  = x^2+5x+6</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice, to arrive at our simplified expression, we took the sum of the
      <q><m>x</m></q>
      terms <m>3x+2x</m> to give us our middle term of <m>5x</m>.
      The constant, <m>6</m>,
      came from the multiplication of the integers from our original expression.
      Our simplified solution is a second-degree expression because it contains an <m>x^2</m> term.
    </p>
    <p>
      To factor an expression of the second degree,
      in the form <m>x^2+bx+c</m>,
      which the coefficient of the <m>x^2</m> term is <m>1</m>,
      we need to find numbers whose sum is the coefficient of the middle term <m>b</m> and whose product is the constant term <m>c</m>.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
              <idx><h>Factor</h><h>Second Degree</h></idx> <em><em>Factoring Second Degree Expressions of the Form <m>x^2+bx+c</m></em></em>
          <ul>
            <li>
              
              <p>
                Consider all the pairs of integers whose product is <m>c</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              
              <p>
                If the sum of any of the pairs is <m>b</m>,
                then the expression can be factored to
                <m>(x+m)(x+n)</m> where <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are the integers.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              
              <p>
                If none of the pairs sum to <m>b</m>,
                then the expression cannot be factored using integers.
                We then say is it
                <q>not factorable over the integers</q>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2+7x+10</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We first consider factors of <m>10</m>, which are <m>10</m> and <m>1</m> (sum is <m>11</m>) or <m>5</m> and <m>2</m> (sum is <m>7</m>). We need the pair we choose to have a sum equal to <m>7</m>, and the only pair that satisfies that is <m>5</m> and <m>2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          So, we can factor <m>x^2+7x+10</m> into <m>(x+5)(x+2)</m>. Remember that multiplication is commutative, so the order of the integers <m>5</m> and <m>2</m> works either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can check our work by using the distributive law:
          <md>
            <mrow>(x+5)(x+2) \amp = x(x)+x(2)+5(x)+5(2)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = x^2+2x+5x+10</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = x^2+7x+10</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2-11x+10</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We know from <xref ref="SecondDegreeFactorExample1" /> that the factors of <m>10</m> to consider are <m>10</m> and <m>1</m> (sum <m>11</m>) or <m>5</m> and <m>2</m> (sum <m>7</m>). However, we are now trying to find a pair whose sum is equal to <m>-11</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Remember that the product of two negative numbers is positive, which means we can also consider the factors of <m>10</m> as <m>-10</m> and <m>-1</m> (sum <m>-11</m>) or <m>-5</m> and <m>-2</m> (sum <m>-7</m>). We need our pair to have a sum of <m>-11</m>, so we choose <m>-10</m> and <m>-1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can factor <m>x^2-11x+10</m> into <m>(x-10)(x-1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2-7x-30</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We now need to find the factors of <m>-30</m>. Remember that only multiplying a negative number by a positive number can give us a negative product. Let's examine the possibilities:
        </p>
        <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
          <col halign="center" />
          <col halign="center" />
          <col halign="center" />
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor">Factors</cell>
            <cell right="minor">Sum</cell>
            <cell right="minor">Product</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30, 1</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-29</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"><m>-15, 2</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-13</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor" bg="yellow">
            <cell right="minor"><m>\mathbf{-10, 3}</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>\mathbf{-7}</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>\mathbf{-30}</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"><m>-6, 5</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-1</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"><m>-5, 6</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"><m>-3, 10</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>7</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"><m>-2, 15</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>13</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor"><m>-1, 30</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>29</m></cell>
            <cell right="minor"><m>-30</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          The factors <m>-10</m> and <m>3</m> give us the sum and product that we are looking for. Therefore, we can factor <m>x^2-7x-30</m> into <m>(x-10)(x+3)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>2x^2+16x+30</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is important to recognize here that the coefficient of the <m>x^2</m> term does not equal <m>1</m>. We can, however, begin by factoring out a common factor, which in this case is <m>2</m>. Once we do that, we are left with:
          <me>
            2x^2+16x+30=2(x^2+8x+15)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Remember that we want to be sure we have factored our expression completely. We must then attempt to factor the expression inside the parentheses. We are looking for factors of <m>15</m> whose sum is <m>8</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Checking the factors of <m>15</m>: <m>15</m> and <m>1</m> (sum <m>16</m>), or <m>5</m> and <m>3</m> (sum <m>8</m>). We need the pair <m>5</m> and <m>3</m>. We can then factor <m>2x^2+16x+30</m> into <m>2(x+5)(x+3)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor: <m>x^3+x^2-6x</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          You will notice that this expression is not second degree because of the presence of the <m>x^3</m>; this is a third degree expression. We can, however, use the same ideas to factor this expression by first looking for a common factor between the three terms.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no common factor between the coefficients other than <m>1</m>. For the literal part, the highest power of <m>x</m> that is a factor of <m>x^3</m>, <m>x^2</m>, and <m>x</m> is <m>x</m>. Therefore, the common factor is <m>x</m>. Once we factor that out, we are left with:
          <me>
            x^3+x^2-6x=x(x^2+x-6)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          To factor completely, we need factors of <m>-6</m> whose sum is <m>1</m> (remember that the middle term, <m>x</m>, is equivalent to <m>1x</m>). The pairs to consider are:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p><m>6, -1</m> (sum <m>5</m>)</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>-6, 1</m> (sum <m>-5</m>)</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>-3, 2</m> (sum <m>-1</m>)</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>3, -2</m> (sum <m>1</m>)</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          We need the pair <m>3, -2</m>. Therefore, we can factor <m>x^3+x^2-6x</m> into <m>x(x+3)(x-2)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample6">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor: <m>x^2-25</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Notice that there are only <m>2</m> terms in this expression, which suggests the coefficient of the <q>middle term</q> must be <m>0</m>. We can think of this expression as <m>x^2+0x-25</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can factor this by finding factors of <m>-25</m> whose sum is <m>0</m>. Our options are:
          <ul>
            <li><m>25, -1</m> (sum <m>24</m>)</li>
            <li><m>-25, 1</m> (sum <m>-24</m>)</li>
            <li><m>-5, 5</m> (sum <m>0</m>)</li>
          </ul>
          We need the pair <m>5, -5</m>. Therefore, we factor <m>x^2-25</m> into <m>(x+5)(x-5)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Checking with the distributive law:
          <md>
            <mrow>(x+5)(x-5) \amp = x(x)+x(-5)+5(x)+5(-5)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = x^2-5x+5x-25</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = x^2-25</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The middle terms simplify to <m>0</m> because the integers used were opposites. When we have a second degree expression in the form <m>a^2-b^2</m>, we call it the <term>difference of squares</term> <idx><h>Factor</h><h>Difference of Squares</h></idx> and factor it into <m>(a+b)(a-b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample7">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor: <m>t^2-36</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Because <m>t \cdot t = t^2</m> and <m>6 \cdot 6 = 36</m>, we can follow the difference of squares pattern to factor <m>t^2-36</m> into <m>(t+6)(t-6)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
    <algorithm xml:id="SecondDegreeFactorExample8">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor: <m>2w^2-32</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          We begin by factoring out the greatest common factor of <m>2</m>:
          <me>
            2(w^2-16)
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We can continue to factor the expression inside the parentheses. Since <m>w \cdot w = w^2</m> and <m>4 \cdot 4 = 16</m>, the binomial is a difference of squares. We factor <m>2w^2-32</m> into <m>2(w+4)(w-4)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>
  </introduction>
  <subsection>
    <title>Exercises</title>
    
    <exercises><exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^2+5x+6</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(x+2)(x+3)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>a^2-a-6</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>g^2+g-6</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(g+3)(g-2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>r^2-4r-5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^2+9x+14</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(x+7)(x+2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^2-12x+36</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^2+9x+18</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(x+3)(x+6)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>y^2+7y-30</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>t^2+9t+20</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(t+5)(t+4)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>g^2-13g+36</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^2+10x-24</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(x+12)(x-2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>h^2+h-30</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f^2-7f+12</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(f-3)(f-4)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>v^2+14v+48</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>r^2-10r-24</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(r-12)(r+2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>p^2-26p+48</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>2b^2+10b+12</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>2(b+3)(b+2)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>2w^2+12w+10</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>5x^2-50x+125</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>5(x-5)(x-5)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^3+3x^2-10x</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>2w^3-4w^2+2w</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>2w(w-1)(w-1)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^2-100</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>a^2-49</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(a+7)(a-7)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>3x^2-75</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>4x^2-36</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>4(x-3)(x+3)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>q^2-225</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>2p^2-72</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>2(p+6)(p-6)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>r^2-121</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
  </exercises></subsection>
</section>
